window.WFD_DOCS_SEARCH=[{"title": "Getting started", "url": "getting-started/", "description": "Power up, choose an integration and connect your first bike.", "text": " Turn the display on, choose your vehicle integration, and connect to your bike. Start with the browser simulator if you want to learn the screens before installing firmware. Screenshots in these guides are software previews with simulated values. They show the interface, not a measured ride or a completed hardware acceptance test. What you need A supported Waveshare ESP32-S3 Touch AMOLED 2.06-inch or GUITION JC4880P443C 4.3-inch board, and a USB data cable. A VESC-based bike running Wheelie Fun Bike, wheel-e, or a compatible generic VESC setup. The firmware image matching your exact board. Use the browser installer for the first USB installation, or follow building and installing for PlatformIO. The Waveshare target is 410 × 502 pixels with CO5300 display and FT3168 touch. GUITION is 480 × 800 with ST7701S display and GT911 touch; its ESP32-C6 companion needs compatible radio firmware. See Waveshare setup and USB recovery or the GUITION setup and silicon revision requirements before installation. Firmware for different boards and revisions is not interchangeable. 1. Power up Connect USB power and let the dashboard appear. If a lock PIN was previously configured, unlock the display before continuing. With no saved bike, Disconnected is normal while the Bike page fills with nearby devices. A saved connection may show Reconnecting . Tap the cog in the upper-right corner to open Settings. Set your preferred brightness, speed units and temperature units. Settings > Orientation cycles through 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°; touch coordinates rotate with the interface. These preferences are saved across restarts. 2. Choose the bike integration Open Bike > Vehicle integration before connecting. Auto is the default: it probes discovered motor controllers for the supported WFB and wheel-e packages and uses the first verified match. This works over BLE, CAN and UART, including renamed devices. Choose a specific integration when you want to require that package; generic VESC provides read-only standard telemetry. Integration What to expect Wheelie Fun Bike Dashboard, ride-response presets, speed-limit controls and supported bike locking. wheel-e Dashboard and supported bike locking. Configure wheel-e tuning through VESC Tool. VESC Standard telemetry and local display features. Package-specific tuning and bike locking are unavailable. The branding and available controls follow the integration. A matching name only affects discovery ranking; it does not establish which package is installed. Auto verifies package replies before enabling integration-specific features. 3. Connect BLE is the default. On the Bike page, select your device. Recognizable bike names appear first, while other VESC UART devices remain selectable. If the upstream device requires encrypted BLE, enter its six-digit pairing PIN when prompted. The display then discovers the main VESC using CAN ping and firmware/package queries. It uses the first matching motor controller with the custom package installed. This controller may be the one directly connected to BLE, or another controller reached through CAN. Wait for live values before using tuning or bike-lock controls. -- means data is unavailable or stale; it does not mean the measured value is zero. See connections for CAN, UART and phone proxy setup. 4. Learn the screens Ride shows speed, battery, power, pitch and connection state. The layout adapts to orientation. Tune exposes only the controls supported by the selected integration. Draft changes take effect only after Apply and confirmation. Bike contains connection, integration and PIN/lock controls. The cog opens Settings, Wi-Fi, firmware updates and About. Swipe horizontally between Ride, Tune and Bike. Vertical drags scroll lists and longer pages. A short horizontal swipe returns to the current page; there is no wrap from Bike back to Ride. 5. Add a lock PIN, if wanted On the Bike page, choose Set lock PIN , enter six digits and confirm them. There is no default lock PIN. Keep your code available; there is no recovery-code interface. Lock screen protects the display controls. Lock bike and screen also requests the package's supported lock command. The display waits for matching telemetry before reporting the bike lock as confirmed. A locked display dims to black after its selected timeout; tap once to wake it, then enter the PIN. The wake tap is consumed. The bike PIN also protects the phone BLE proxy when enabled. An upstream VESC Express pairing PIN is separate and does not become the proxy PIN. See PINs and locking . 6. Keep it up to date Open Settings > Wi-Fi , select a network and enter its password. Then open Settings > Firmware updates , check the release, and confirm installation. Keep power connected while updating; restart only when offered. An image must match the board and pass validation. If no public release is available, the display reports that without replacing the installed firmware. See updates and maintenance . Where next Use the display · Connections and phone proxy · Troubleshooting · Build the firmware · Contribute "}, {"title": "Using the display", "url": "guide/", "description": "Ride, Tune, Bike, gestures and everyday settings.", "text": " The main screens keep the ride information close and the configuration controls deliberate. The same interface works at 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°. Tap Settings > Orientation to advance through those angles; the selection is saved across restarts. Ride Speed uses your saved km/h or mph preference. Temperature units are configured separately. Pitch is shown with the degree symbol. The dashboard uses the selected integration's live VESC data; it does not calculate a substitute speed when messages are missing. Connection setup and data freshness are separate. A Bluetooth or wired link can be established before valid telemetry arrives. Readings become -- when they expire, and configuration controls stay unavailable until their requirements are met. Tune WFB offers Eco, Cruise and Sport ride-response presets plus speed-limit controls. Select a draft, tap Apply, review the confirmation and apply the change while the bike is stopped. The firmware checks connection identity, freshness, speed and controller fault again before enqueueing the command. A queued tuning command is not a readback. The current protocol does not document an acknowledgement for these writes. Selecting a preset in the UI does not prove it is the bike's stored preset. Wheel-e and generic VESC do not show WFB-only tuning controls. Use VESC Tool for configuration that this display does not expose. Bike Leave the integration at Auto (the default) to detect a supported package through controller probes. Choose a specific integration to require that package. Disconnect or forget the bike to change the selection. The main-controller discovery status explains which controller the display is using; Find main controller again reruns the read-only discovery process when the link is available. Lock controls and the locked-screen timeout also live here. Unsupported package features are not enabled by selecting a different page. Settings The cog is always in the upper-right header. The Settings list scrolls vertically. Setting Behavior Orientation Rotates the interface and touch mapping together. Speed units Switches km/h and mph. Temperature units Switches °C and °F independently of speed units. Brightness Sets the brightness restored after waking the locked display. Vehicle connection Selects BLE, CAN or UART when wired interfaces are configured for the target. BLE name Changes this display's own advertised name; default WB Disp . VESC BLE proxy Enables the phone connection; off by default. Wi-Fi Scans, connects, forgets a network or enters a hidden SSID. Firmware updates Checks and installs a matching GitHub release image. About Shows version, board identity and live diagnostics. Touch and gestures Horizontal swipes change the three primary pages and follow your finger. Short or reversed drags snap back. Vertical motion scrolls Tune and Bike content rather than changing pages. Moving pages do not accept accidental Apply or device-selection taps. The layout keeps content inside the panel's rounded corners. Changing orientation cancels a held gesture; lift your finger before starting another touch. The keyboard and PIN entry also respect the safe area. When a phone is connected The phone owns the VESC conversation while it is connected to the proxy. The display suspends its own queries, configuration writes and firmware maintenance. Recognized replies may update the dashboard passively; other readings become stale. Disconnect the phone to return the conversation to the display. The phone chooses its own CAN controller. The proxy never substitutes the display's selected main controller. See connections . "}, {"title": "Connections & proxy", "url": "connections/", "description": "BLE, CAN, UART and your phone's transparent VESC connection.", "text": " BLE, CAN and UART share the vehicle integration layer. Choose the physical interface in Settings; choose the package integration on the Bike page. Bluetooth to the bike BLE is available by default. The Bike picker prioritizes recognizable names without excluding other VESC Nordic UART Service devices. Select the device, complete any pairing prompt, and wait for main-controller discovery and fresh telemetry. The saved association includes the verified BLE peer and selected integration. Bluetooth bonds are separate from that association. If an upstream device changes its pairing credentials, retry pairing with the new PIN rather than changing the bike lock PIN. This pairing preview uses a simulated browser service; it does not establish radio interoperability. Finding the main controller For BLE, the display sends the standard VESC CAN ping command, then queries firmware and package telemetry for the direct controller and CAN candidates. Native CAN performs CAN ping and firmware queries on the bus. A BMS is not selected as a motor controller. The first compatible motor controller with a supported custom package installed is selected automatically. With Auto (the default), discovery probes both WFB and wheel-e packages and uses a verified reply, independently of the BLE name. A manual package selection requires that package to respond. No controller-choice prompt is required. Auto falls back to read-only standard VESC telemetry only when no supported package is found; generic VESC can also be selected explicitly. Discovery is read-only. Tuning and lock requests wait until the route, connection generation and required telemetry are valid. Discovery repeats after the connection changes; old queued work is not replayed onto another controller. Native CAN The ESP32-S3 has a TWAI controller but needs an external CAN transceiver. Use the board schematic and the transceiver's electrical requirements when choosing GPIOs and wiring. The display's CAN identity must be unique on the bus. The firmware exposes CAN in Settings > Vehicle connection only when both CAN_TX and CAN_RX are defined in that target's PlatformIO build flags. Defaults are 500 kbit/s and display node ID 254. The target motor-controller ID is discovered, not hard-coded into a bike command. UART UART appears only when both UART_TX and UART_RX are configured for the target. Match the vehicle's UART baud setting; the default is 115200. Vehicle UART defaults to port 1; port 0 remains reserved for the console. Use compatible logic levels, cross TX/RX as required, and share a signal ground. GPIO assignments are target configuration, not a setting that can be guessed from the screen model. See building . Connect a phone through the display Enable Settings > VESC BLE proxy . It is off by default. Find the display in your phone app using its BLE name , initially WB Disp . If a bike lock PIN is configured, pair using that six-digit PIN. The upstream device's pairing PIN is not reused. In the phone app, perform its normal VESC discovery and choose the controller you want to use. Disconnect the phone when you want the display to resume its own polling or configuration. The proxy supports the selected BLE, UART or CAN interface. BLE and UART preserve the underlying VESC byte stream. CAN exposes a generic adapter with firmware discovery, CAN ping and explicit CAN forwarding; the phone supplies the destination CAN ID. The proxy does not use the display's main-controller route. The advertised name belongs to this display. Changing vehicle integration or connecting to a differently named BLE module does not rename it. Edit Settings > BLE name when you want a different name. Pairing and changed PINs An encrypted upstream BLE connection has its own PIN prompt and saved bond. The local bike PIN determines the proxy's BLE pairing requirement. Changing or removing the bike PIN invalidates the old phone security sessions and bonds, so reconnect or re-pair the phone afterward. PIN pairing and physical CAN/UART behavior still require acceptance testing with the intended controller and phone. See open work for the remaining hardware checks. "}, {"title": "PINs & locking", "url": "pins/", "description": "Separate upstream pairing from the local bike and proxy PIN.", "text": " There are two separate PIN purposes: connecting this display to a secured BLE device, and protecting this display plus its phone proxy. PIN Where it comes from What it controls Upstream BLE pairing PIN The VESC Express or other secured bike BLE device This display's encrypted connection to that device. Bike lock PIN Six digits set in the Bike menu Local lock screen, supported bike lock requests and the display's phone BLE proxy. The upstream pairing PIN never becomes the proxy PIN. Set or change the bike PIN Open Bike > Set lock PIN , enter six digits and confirm them. If a PIN already exists, Change PIN and Remove PIN require the current code first. No default PIN or recovery-code screen is provided. The persistent record uses a salted PIN digest. The numeric proxy passkey is held in RAM after PIN setup or successful local unlock. Boot with a configured PIN requires local unlock before the secured proxy becomes available; the upstream pairing PIN itself is not saved. Lock only the screen Lock screen protects the local controls without sending a bike lock command. The locked-screen timeout can be 15, 30, 60 or 120 seconds, with 30 seconds as the default. When it times out, the panel becomes black. Tap once to restore your brightness setting, lift your finger, and enter the PIN. That first tap cannot activate a hidden control. Partial PIN input is cleared across sleep and wake. Lock the bike and screen When supported, Lock bike and screen requests the package's explicit LOCK command. The bike must have fresh stationary telemetry, no controller fault and a ready exclusive connection. The screen stays locked while the display waits for matching telemetry from the same connection. A command being queued is not proof that propulsion is disabled. If the controller does not confirm the state, the interface reports it as unconfirmed. Validate lock behavior on the exact bike firmware before relying on it. The supplied WFB protocol notes do not establish vendor firmware compatibility by themselves. Pair the phone With no bike PIN, the enabled proxy has no PIN requirement. With a bike PIN, the phone must complete authenticated BLE pairing using that code. Changing or removing the code disconnects old security sessions and removes old phone bonds; pair again when prompted. Upstream bike bonds are independent. Changing the local PIN does not change the PIN configured on VESC Express. Keep the security boundaries clear The local code protects this display's controls and its proxy. It does not prevent another client from talking directly to the bike's separate interface, and it is not a hardware immobilizer. Physical flash access is outside this local access-control mechanism. See locking internals for implementation details and open work for remaining hardware acceptance checks. "}, {"title": "Updates & maintenance", "url": "maintenance/", "description": "Install updates, diagnose issues and prepare release artifacts.", "text": " Keep firmware artifacts tied to the hardware profile and record the checks performed when reviewing a release. Update from the display Open Settings > Wi-Fi and connect to a network. The keyboard supports hidden SSIDs and password entry. Open Settings > Firmware updates and check for a release. Review the available version, confirm installation and keep the display powered. Restart when the verified image is ready. The public release feed is techfoundrynz/wheelie_fun_display . A release needs the matching environment-named image and GitHub SHA256 digest. Missing releases or incompatible images are reported without replacing the installed firmware. Installation pauses vehicle communications. A phone owning the proxy conversation blocks maintenance until it disconnects. Certificate-verified HTTPS and image validation protect the download; this is not a separate signed-firmware/secure-boot scheme. Automatic releases and PR previews Increase [common].custom_firmware_version in platformio.ini , commit and push to main . CI compares the version before and after the entire push, including multi-commit pushes. A higher semantic version triggers publication; unrelated configuration edits, formatting changes, downgrades and build-metadata-only changes do not. The pipeline runs formatting, sanitizer tests and native UI checks, builds every supported default_envs target once, then tests the browser simulator and installer using those firmware artifacts. Only after those stages succeed does it create v<version> at that commit and publish the OTA/factory images, notices and source references. An existing tag must identify the same commit; published assets are never replaced. Re-running the same published commit leaves its release unchanged. For the first release, or an explicit manual publication, run Actions > CI, firmware and previews > Run workflow on main with publish_release enabled. A manual run without that option validates only. Pushed version tags also work, but the tag must match the configuration. Prerelease versions are marked as prereleases on GitHub; separate OTA channels are not implemented. PRs run the same validation and produce a hosted simulator link and browser-test gallery in one updated bot comment and the publication run summary. Each URL includes the PR number and exact head revision. Preview frames isolate PR scripts from the parent and disable hardware APIs. Superseded builds are skipped, and closing a PR removes its preview and updates the comment. A separate trusted publisher runs after successful CI, verifies artifact provenance, refreshes only the tested installer's release catalog and firmware files from published releases and deploys Pages. It executes only default-branch publication code, never PR publication scripts. Generated site content is retained on the gh-pages storage branch so production updates preserve open PR previews. The first successful main pipeline initializes that storage automatically; PR publication cannot replace an uninitialized production site. Downloadable site and firmware artifacts remain available as a fallback. Release artifacts Build the supported environments before packaging: pio run python scripts/check_sdkconfig.py --all python -m pip install -r requirements-release.txt python scripts/package_release.py Packaging writes OTA applications .pio/releases/<environment>.bin , complete USB images <environment>-factory.bin , manifest.json , licenses.zip and source-references.json . The notices archive preserves the project, font, icon and bundled component licenses. Source references record the application repository/commit and pinned Slint tag/commit; local uncommitted changes are marked explicitly. The supported list comes from default_envs ; removed board images are not carried forward. CI builds that supported list at the configured version and passes the verified artifacts to the release stage. The complete USB image is merged from the build's actual bootloader, partition-table, OTA initialization and application offsets; it is not interchangeable with an OTA application image. USB installation can reset NVS settings. Review the version, board identity, sizes and hashes before publishing. App slots are 7 MiB each. A newly booted image is accepted only after initialization and ten seconds of a functioning UI loop; an early failure leaves rollback available. Diagnose before resetting About > Diagnostics provides version/build identity, uptime, memory, display work timings and connection information. Save the serial log and describe the action that caused an issue. Panel work timings are not the same as measured delivered FPS. NVS contains preferences, saved associations, bonds, Wi-Fi details and PIN state. Do not erase it as routine troubleshooting; that changes the user's setup and makes a problem harder to reproduce. Hardware acceptance A successful build and simulator check do not establish radio or wired interoperability. For a release, record the exact board, controller firmware and phone/app used, then exercise: Cold boot, all four orientations, corner-safe layout and touch alignment. Settings scrolling, PIN entry, locked timeout and wake-contact consumption. Disconnect/reconnect, upstream encrypted pairing and changed/removed local PINs. CAN/UART operation with the actual transceiver/wiring and controller discovery. Phone proxy behavior, CAN forwarding, MTU differences, sustained traffic and lease release. Wi-Fi/OTA success, failure, cancellation and first-boot acceptance. Track remaining hardware work in TODO . Record repeatable performance measurements in performance notes ; a startup sample does not establish a sustained frame rate. Documentation and Pages CI builds the browser simulator, renders documentation pictures from that current shared Slint UI, then assembles and tests the site. Screenshots are generated artifacts under .cache/pages/docs-images ; do not check them in or regenerate them before committing. To reproduce the complete Pages build locally: python -m pip install -r requirements-docs.txt -r simulator/requirements-browser-test.txt python -m playwright install chromium python scripts/build_web_simulator.py --output .cache/pages/site/simulator python scripts/generate_docs_screenshots.py --site .cache/pages/site python scripts/build_docs.py --output .cache/pages/site python scripts/build_flash_site.py --site .cache/pages/site --github-release python tests/flasher_browser_test.py --site .cache/pages/site python tests/simulator_browser_test.py --site .cache/pages/site python scripts/package_browser_results.py --site .cache/pages/site python tests/docs_browser_test.py --site .cache/pages/site The browser installer uses pinned ESP Web Tools and same-origin, SHA256-verified release images. After successful CI and any release publication, the trusted Pages publisher refreshes the installer from published images without rebuilding the simulator. Before the first release, the installer lists supported boa"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting/", "description": "Find the right next check for connection, touch or update issues.", "text": " Start with what the display reports, then check the part of the connection it describes. Keep the bike stopped while investigating configuration or locking. The screen is black If the display was locked, tap once, lift your finger and wait for the PIN screen. The timeout intentionally sets brightness to zero; waking restores your saved brightness. For a first installation, confirm the exact board target and that the full USB upload completed. A 1.8-inch or 2.41-inch image is not supported by this repository. Capture the boot log if the correct 2.06-inch build never shows the interface. The USB port is missing Use a USB data cable and try another direct port. Check pio device list . Follow the manufacturer's BOOT/RESET download-mode procedure if the board is not enumerating. Do not assume the port number is the same as a previous session. No bike appears over BLE Power the bike and open its BLE interface. Make sure another client has not occupied a single-connection module. Recognizable bike names sort first, but other VESC UART devices can still appear. Leave the integration at Auto to probe supported packages regardless of the BLE name. If selecting a package explicitly, check that it is installed on the intended motor controller. The link connects but values stay unavailable Transport connection, main-controller discovery and telemetry freshness are separate. Check the main-controller status on the Bike page. The package must match WFB or wheel-e when that integration is selected; generic VESC does not expose package-specific controls. If a phone owns the proxy, the display pauses its own queries. Disconnect the phone and let discovery/polling resume. If needed, choose Find main controller again . -- is an unavailable reading, not zero. The BLE PIN is rejected Use the PIN configured on the upstream BLE device when pairing the display to it. Use the local bike lock PIN when pairing the phone to this display. They are separate codes. If credentials changed, retry pairing with the new upstream PIN. Changing or removing the bike PIN invalidates the phone's old security sessions/bonds. The phone may also need to forget its cached pairing before pairing again. CAN or UART is absent from Settings The target must define both pins for that interface. The stock profile leaves optional wired pins unset. Check the build flags, actual board wiring, CAN transceiver, bus bit rate, unique display CAN ID or UART baud as applicable. Rebuild after changing target configuration. A tuning or lock action is unavailable The selected integration may not support it. Otherwise, wait for fresh stationary telemetry, a ready connection and no controller fault. A phone proxy lease also reserves the conversation. Draft values are not readbacks. For a lock request, an unconfirmed state means the display has not received the required matching telemetry. Do not assume the motor is disabled merely because the request was queued. No update is offered Confirm Wi-Fi and the release feed, and look for a public release containing the exact board asset plus its digest. No release, a missing asset or a validation failure leaves the installed firmware in place. See maintenance . Report a reproducible problem Include the board target, firmware version, integration, transport, controller/phone/app versions, the last working behavior, exact steps and a serial log. Never include Wi-Fi passwords or PINs. See contributing . "}, {"title": "Waveshare S3 2.06-inch", "url": "hardware/waveshare-206/", "description": "AMOLED setup, board wiring, orientation and USB recovery.", "text": " Use the waveshare_esp32s3_206 target for the Waveshare ESP32-S3 Touch AMOLED 2.06 . Its native resolution is 410 × 502, with a CO5300 AMOLED panel, FT3168 touch, 32 MB flash and 8 MB octal PSRAM. Wi-Fi and Bluetooth are built into the ESP32-S3; no radio companion needs provisioning. The picture is generated from the current shared UI during the documentation build. Its connected values are illustrative. Install and build Choose Waveshare ESP32-S3 AMOLED 2.06 in the browser firmware installer , using a USB data cable. Select the exact board model rather than another Waveshare display size. To build this target locally: pio run -e waveshare_esp32s3_206 The application is .pio/build/waveshare_esp32s3_206/firmware.bin . Release packaging produces waveshare_esp32s3_206.bin for OTA and waveshare_esp32s3_206-factory.bin for a complete USB installation. The complete image can reset saved settings; prefer the display's Wi-Fi/OTA flow for an ordinary update. Both supported boards use the common PlatformIO and ESP-IDF pins and the matching, checksum-verified Slint release. Shared settings are in sdkconfig.defaults , S3 settings in config/sdkconfig.s3 , and board capabilities in platformio.ini . No Rust toolchain is needed for firmware builds. Display, touch and orientation The profile runs the CPU at 240 MHz, flash and octal PSRAM at 80 MHz, and the panel's QSPI interface at 80 MHz. It uses internal DMA scanline buffers for rendering, with immutable font metadata kept in flash. CO5300 uses the managed SH8601-compatible QSPI transport with a 22-pixel column offset. FT3168 uses the compatible FT5x06 touch driver. Display and touch resets use direct GPIO control; there is no I/O expander. The rounded panel uses a 32-pixel safe inset. Settings > Orientation cycles through 0°, 90°, 180° and 270° and saves the preference. The layout and touch transform follow the selected orientation; the native panel dimensions remain 410 × 502. Board wiring These are the shipped profile's internal connections, not suggested pins for external accessories. Function GPIO / value I2C SDA / SCL 15 / 14 QSPI clock / chip select 11 / 12 QSPI data 0–3 4 / 5 / 6 / 7 Display reset / enable 8 / 13 Touch reset / interrupt 9 / 38 Panel column offset 22 pixels BLE is the default vehicle transport. CAN and UART appear in Settings only after their pin pairs are configured in the target profile. Choose available pins and provide the required external CAN transceiver or UART wiring; see vehicle transports . USB recovery and checks If the serial device is missing, hold BOOT , tap RESET , then release BOOT . Check the USB data cable and run pio device list to identify the board's current port. After installation, confirm the dashboard, cog, Settings scrolling and touch at all four orientations. The connected S3 board has passed SDK 6.1 startup and rendering checks; real-bike, phone-proxy and OTA acceptance remain listed in TODO . Occasional touch I2C read errors are also tracked there; save the serial log when diagnosing a touch issue. See troubleshooting , performance configuration , the Waveshare product page and vendor examples . "}, {"title": "GUITION P4 4.3-inch", "url": "hardware/guition-43/", "description": "P4/C6 board setup, companion firmware and hardware requirements.", "text": " The guition_jc4880p443c target is for the GUITION JC4880P443C_I_W / enclosed _Y board, described as JC4880P433 in the original article. It has a 480 × 800 ST7701S MIPI-DSI panel, GT911 touch, 16 MB flash, 32 MB PSRAM and an ESP32-C6 radio companion. The supplied profile selects ESP32-P4 revisions below 3.0 , including the original v1.3 engineering-sample board. ESP-IDF treats those chips and production v3.x chips as incompatible targets. Do not use this profile on a v3.x board; check the boot log or vendor revision before installation. Build This board is included in the supported default_envs . To build it on its own: pio run -e guition_jc4880p443c The pinned Slint release supplies matching compiler and MCU runtime assets for ESP32-S3 and ESP32-P4. Firmware builds download and verify those assets; a local Rust runtime bootstrap is not required. Both profiles use the common PlatformIO Espressif32 8.0.0 and ESP-IDF 6.1 pins. The P4 profile selects pre-v3 silicon and its qualified 360 MHz CPU setting. Shared settings live in sdkconfig.defaults ; architecture-specific values live in config/ . Managed component versions are pinned once in firmware/main/idf_component.yml ; each chip has its own generated dependency lock. For a published release, the browser installer flashes the complete P4 image. It does not replace the C6 companion firmware; prepare that separately as described below. Display and touch Signal Configuration Native panel 480 × 800, RGB565, two DSI lanes DSI PHY LDO channel 3 at 2.5 V; 500 Mbit/s per lane DPI pixel clock 34 MHz Horizontal timing sync 12, back porch 42, front porch 42 Vertical timing sync 2, back porch 8, front porch 166 Panel reset GPIO5 Backlight GPIO23, LEDC brightness control Touch I2C SDA GPIO7, SCL GPIO8, 400 kHz Touch reset GPIO3 Touch addressing Probe 0x5D, then 0x14; polling without an interrupt pin Slint renders into a persistent, full-screen RGB565 canvas in PSRAM. The P4 pixel processing accelerator (PPA) rotates each completed frame into the inactive native DSI framebuffer; two native framebuffers keep scanout separate from rendering. Presentation waits for refresh completion before reusing the old front buffer. This uses three full-screen allocations (2.30 MB total), with no internal-RAM scanline buffers. All four display orientations use PPA rotation and the matching native-coordinate touch transforms. PSRAM runs at 200 MHz. The supplied profile starts at 90° (800 × 480 landscape). DISPLAY_ROTATION sets the initial angle in the hardware profile; runtime selection is saved in NVS. ESP32-C6 radio firmware The P4 has no built-in radio. Wi-Fi and both BLE roles use the C6 through ESP-Hosted: standard esp_wifi calls are supplied by esp_wifi_remote , and the P4 NimBLE host communicates with the C6 Bluetooth controller through the Hosted HCI tunnel. P4-to-C6 signal P4 GPIO SDIO slot 1, four-bit bus, 25 MHz CLK / CMD 18 / 19 D0 / D1 / D2 / D3 14 / 15 / 16 / 17 Companion reset 54 The target pins esp_hosted 3.1.0 and esp_wifi_remote 1.6.5. The C6 must run compatible ESP-Hosted firmware with SDIO transport, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth controller HCI enabled. An arbitrary vendor Wi-Fi demo is not sufficient. The P4 application image does not overwrite or automatically replace the C6 firmware. Hosted 3.x MCU hosts also support compatible older Hosted streaming/1.x/2.x companion images, so an existing image may work if those radio features are enabled. A matching 3.1.0 companion is recommended; verify its firmware and configuration before replacing it. Use the matching ESP-Hosted 3.1.0 examples/bluetooth/esp_hosted_nimble/bleprph_wifi_coex/cp project when preparing the companion. Hosted 3.x replaces the old slave/ project with per-example cp/ projects. This coexistence profile enables Wi-Fi, controller-only Bluetooth and HCI over the Hosted transport. Build it for esp32c6 using ESP-IDF 6.1, and verify SDIO and the board's C6 wiring in menuconfig before building. Flash it only through the C6's documented programming connection, not the P4 application port. Follow the board vendor's instructions for C6 boot mode and connector access. After provisioning, test a normal BLE vehicle connection, encrypted pairing, phone proxy connection, Wi-Fi and OTA together. Verify the display stays responsive during flash writes and that no DSI underruns appear in the serial log. Those checks require this physical board and a compatible C6 image. Scope Camera, audio, microSD, battery telemetry and RS-485 are not initialized by this application. Optional vehicle CAN/UART pins can be configured in the hardware profile after wiring the required interface; they are not guessed from expansion-header availability. The radio SDIO bus must remain reserved for the C6. Sources and notices GUITION product specifications . ESP-IDF P4 revision selection . Espressif hosted NimBLE/VHCI example . Espressif Hosted migration and companion compatibility . Espressif hosted companion project . MIT-licensed board initialization reference . Its panel register table is retained with the original copyright and MIT notice in firmware/main/boards/ ; the display lifecycle and rendering integration are implemented in this project. "}, {"title": "Build & install", "url": "building/", "description": "Set up PlatformIO, install firmware and run the shared simulators.", "text": " The firmware uses ESP-IDF through PlatformIO and a pinned Slint compiler/runtime pair. Firmware builds use matching prebuilt MCU compiler/runtime assets. Desktop/browser simulators need Rust. Both supported targets share the pinned platform and SDK in the common PlatformIO configuration. Supported hardware Environment Native resolution Hardware waveshare_esp32s3_206 410 × 502 Waveshare ESP32-S3 Touch AMOLED 2.06, CO5300 / FT3168, 32 MB flash guition_jc4880p443c 480 × 800 GUITION JC4880P443C, ST7701S / GT911, 16 MB flash, P4 + C6 See the Waveshare S3 2.06-inch setup guide for its board wiring and USB recovery. The GUITION P4 4.3-inch target requires an ESP32-P4 below revision 3.0 and compatible C6 companion firmware. Both targets are included in default_envs . PlatformIO configuration is the source of truth. Do not flash a different Waveshare size or revision with this target merely because it also uses an ESP32-S3. Prepare the tools Install Python and PlatformIO Core, then use a terminal at the repository root. CI pins PlatformIO Core 6.2.0. python -m pip install platformio==6.2.0 pio run python scripts/check_sdkconfig.py --all pio run builds all supported default_envs . The project pins Espressif platform 8.0.0 / ESP-IDF 6.1 in the common environment and a matching pinned Slint compiler/runtime pair. The Slint compiler and runtime are downloaded together with checksum verification. Each application image is .pio/build/<environment>/firmware.bin . The ESP project identity is derived as wfd_<hardware-id> ; release filenames use the PlatformIO environment name. First USB installation For a published release, open the browser installer , choose the exact display and connect with a USB data cable. The installer checks the chip family and flashes a complete matching image. It can reset saved settings; use OTA for normal updates. The P4 installer does not provision the C6 companion. When uploading a local build with PlatformIO, discover the port rather than copying someone else's COM number: pio device list pio run -e waveshare_esp32s3_206 -t upload --upload-port YOUR_PORT pio device monitor --port YOUR_PORT Replace YOUR_PORT with the detected port, such as COM9 on Windows or /dev/ttyACM0 on Linux. If the board is not detected, use a known USB data cable and enter the board's download mode with BOOT/RESET as described by its manufacturer. The first installation must include the matching bootloader, partition table, OTA initialization data and application. PlatformIO upload uses the configured target layout. Do not upload only an application image to a factory partition layout. After installation, verify the dashboard, touch, all four orientations and the boot log before connecting a bike. Configure an optional wired interface BLE is the default. CAN and UART are available only when their pin pairs are defined in the target's build_flags . Choose unused, exposed GPIOs from the actual board schematic; the repository does not guess wiring. Build flag Default / requirement CAN_TX , CAN_RX Both required to enable CAN. Connect through an external transceiver. CAN_BITRATE 500000 ; supported values 125000, 250000, 500000 and 1000000. CAN_LOCAL_ID 254 ; must be unique on the vehicle CAN bus. UART_TX , UART_RX Both required to enable UART. UART_BAUD 115200 ; must match the vehicle. BIKE_UART_PORT 1 ; ports 1 and 2 are supported, not the console port 0. Add the chosen -D definitions to the hardware profile and rebuild. The interfaces then appear in Settings. If a saved interface is unavailable in the current build, the display falls back to BLE. The main motor controller is found through discovery rather than a target CAN-ID build flag. Editor and live preview python scripts/sync_slint_lsp.py This downloads the pinned language server/live preview and writes the machine-specific path to ignored VS Code settings. Reload the editor after initial setup. The tracked shared settings remain portable; do not commit generated absolute tool paths. Desktop simulator Install a Rust toolchain, then run: cargo run --locked --manifest-path simulator/Cargo.toml cargo run --locked --manifest-path simulator/Cargo.toml -- 502 410 cargo run --locked --manifest-path simulator/Cargo.toml -- --wired cargo run --locked --manifest-path simulator/Cargo.toml -- --smoke-test The --wired demo exposes simulated CAN/UART settings. Simulators use fake bike/Wi-Fi data and never drive hardware or download firmware. The browser simulator runs the actual shared Slint UI and software renderer in WebAssembly. Application services remain browser-only mocks: connections, Wi-Fi, OTA, PIN validation and pairing do not reach hardware. Demo PINs/preferences reset on reload; the demo upstream pairing PIN is 123456 . Serve the generated site over HTTP rather than opening it with file:// . Host checks On Linux, or in WSL with a C++ compiler, CMake and Ninja: cmake -S tests -B .cache/host-tests -G Ninja -DBIKE_SANITIZERS=ON cmake --build .cache/host-tests ctest --test-dir .cache/host-tests --output-on-failure On Windows, use an installed compiler generator and omit sanitizer flags if unsupported. Headless UI checks use the shared Slint screens: cargo run --locked --manifest-path simulator/Cargo.toml --bin transition-checks -- 410 502 cargo run --locked --manifest-path simulator/Cargo.toml --bin transition-checks -- 502 410 See contributing for choosing checks appropriate to a change and maintenance for release packaging. "}, {"title": "Contributing", "url": "contributing/", "description": "Make a focused change and show the evidence that it works.", "text": " Changes should preserve the separation between vehicle integrations, physical transports, local access control and UI rendering. A new board or protocol should not add board-name branches throughout shared code. Start with a reproducible change For a bug, describe the concrete trigger, observed behavior and expected result. Include the board environment, firmware version, integration and transport. For BLE or proxy issues, include controller and phone/app versions. Redact PINs, passwords and other credentials from logs. Discuss substantial new hardware/protocol support before assuming undocumented wire formats. The protocol references record compatibility details and uncertainties; keep those distinctions visible. Repository map Area Responsibility ui/ Shared Slint components, layout, gestures and dialogs. firmware/main/ Board drivers, runtime ownership, transports, networking and application projection. firmware/components/bike/ Portable framing, integrations, telemetry and state policies. simulator/ Desktop, browser and headless previews of the shared UI. tests/ Portable C++ checks for protocol, state and renderer-related policies. scripts/ Reproducible configuration, packaging and documentation helpers. docs/ Rider guides, architecture and maintenance documentation. Keep changes reviewable Put target capabilities, resolution and GPIOs in the PlatformIO hardware profile. Share settings through common rather than inheriting one board from another. Put package-specific encoding and feature flags in its vehicle integration. Unsupported commands must be gated in both UI and command handling. Keep the phone proxy independent of the display's selected main-controller route. Invalidate old RX/TX work across connection or transport generations. Do not replay a tuning command after reconnect. Keep Slint ownership on the UI task. Communication callbacks should transfer bounded data, not edit UI properties. Preserve required third-party notices and source/binary line-ending rules. Validate the change Use the checks that establish the behavior you changed. Firmware changes normally need a build and SDK audit: pio run python scripts/check_sdkconfig.py --all Portable protocol/state changes need host tests; see building . UI changes should be checked at 410 × 502 and 502 × 410 with the headless transition checks and actual rendered fixtures. Exercise touch/drag cancellation, safe corners and orientation as relevant. Run cargo fmt --check --manifest-path simulator/Cargo.toml for Rust changes. C/C++ formatting uses the repository .clang-format plus declaration spacing; CI pins clang-format 23.1.0. Run python scripts/format_cpp.py to format, or add --check to verify without changes. Do not write a test that simply restates the implementation without exercising the failure or behavior that matters. For docs, build the Pages artifact and inspect desktop and mobile layouts. The browser simulator is a demo: it must never imply that mocked controls operate a real vehicle. Describe evidence precisely A useful change description states the problem, resulting behavior and relevant validation. Distinguish a compiler check, host/simulator test, startup log and a real-controller acceptance test. If radio, PIN changes or wired behavior could not be tested, say so and leave the acceptance item open. Do not claim delivered FPS from a first-frame timing or successful boot. Add reproducible performance measurements to the performance notes . License Project contributions use GPL-3.0-only, subject to the existing third-party notices. See LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY.md . Do not copy images, fonts or code into the repository without retaining their applicable permissions and notices. Pull request checks and previews PR CI checks C++/Python/Rust formatting, sanitizer-backed protocol/transport tests, all four UI viewport layouts and touch rotations, both firmware targets, browser interactions and installer integrity. A successful run publishes an isolated simulator preview and browser-test gallery, linked by one updated bot comment. New commits replace the preview; closed PRs have it removed. Downloadable artifacts remain available from the run. Production deployment and PR preview publication use trusted default-branch code after the read-only build succeeds. The publisher verifies the run, head revision and current PR state before deploying. Version increases on main additionally create an immutable release after every validation stage passes; see release maintenance . "}, {"title": "Architecture", "url": "architecture/", "description": "Ownership, integrations, transports and shared UI components.", "text": " The application uses ESP-IDF, PlatformIO and a pinned Slint runtime. Board integration, standard VESC BLE transport, protocol handling, state management and UI are implemented in this repository. Layer Location Responsibility UI ui/ Declarative responsive layout, theme, input and confirmation Application firmware/main/main.cpp Polling, session tracking, state freshness, settings authorization and UI projection Protocol firmware/components/bike/ Shared VESC framing, CRC, vehicle integrations and transactional telemetry decoding BLE firmware/main/ble.cpp NimBLE central lifecycle, GATT discovery, bounded queues and ordered transmission Board firmware/main/board.cpp , board_guition_p4.cpp Profile-based power/reset, QSPI or MIPI-DSI display, touch and brightness Renderer firmware/main/slint_backend.cpp , slint_renderer_*.cpp Single-task Slint ownership, touch dispatch and target-specific rendering/presentation Runtime packaging firmware/components/slint_runtime/ Matching prebuilt compiler, headers and MCU library Data flow and ownership NimBLE runs its host task. Notification callbacks copy bounded fragments into a 12-entry queue. A dedicated transmission task serializes VESC frame chunks, using negotiated ATT MTU minus three bytes and a bounded retry on controller-memory exhaustion. Queue overflow or a failed partial transmission disconnects rather than continuing a damaged byte stream. The application/UI task consumes receive fragments, checks connection generation, feeds the VESC decoder, updates telemetry and dispatches Slint input/rendering. UI properties are never touched from BLE callbacks. A mutex protects transport snapshots; bounded queues carry cross-task work. Old-generation RX and TX work is discarded after reconnect. Decoder and telemetry state are reset whenever the connection is not ready. The application requests COMM_GET_VALUES_SETUP and the selected integration's package telemetry at 5 Hz. WFB uses GET_ALL_DATA(mode=1) ; wheel-e uses REALTIME_DATA with state, pitch and roll masks. Generic VESC polls only standard telemetry. Poll cadence is independent of the untrusted reported loop time. Setup values provide speed in m/s (signed integer / 1000), battery and temperatures; package replies supply attitude and state. Unknown responses and replies for another package are ignored. Short/malformed replies cannot partially overwrite an existing sample. Setup and package freshness expire independently after 1500 ms. CAN interrupt ownership The CAN adapter uses ESP-IDF's handle-based TWAI driver. Eight persistent transmit slots retain frame descriptors and payloads until completion; a bounded internal queue copies received frames out of the interrupt context. Completion and fault queues transfer ownership to the wired worker, which alone changes slot state and runs protocol/discovery logic. ISR callbacks use FreeRTOS FromISR operations, avoiding compiler atomic helpers on Xtensa builds that disable hardware atomics. Keep interrupt callback definitions out of line with IRAM_ATTR ; inline class-body callbacks can produce unreachable COMDAT literal pools on Xtensa. The driver tests compile the production adapter against controlled SDK fakes to exercise early completion, buffer lifetime, queue overflow, failure and cleanup. Hardware CAN acceptance remains separate; see transports . Changes to bike settings The rider chooses a draft, opens a confirmation, then explicitly applies it. The application rechecks the session, a 30-second confirmation expiry, fresh standard and selected-package telemetry, controller fault zero, and absolute speed below 0.2 m/s immediately before enqueueing a command. Pending confirmations are cancelled on disconnect. There is no automatic tune or mode write at startup/reconnect, and no motor-drive command path. This is a display-side interlock, not a motor safety system; the controller remains responsible for accepting settings appropriately. BLE write-without-response and the documented live commands provide no proof of application. The UI distinguishes queueing from confirmation by the controller. Display and memory 2.06 wiring: I2C SDA15/SCL14; QSPI CS12/CLK11/D0-D3 4-7; display reset GPIO8 and enable GPIO13; touch reset GPIO9 and interrupt GPIO38. CO5300 uses the managed SH8601 QSPI transport with a 22-pixel column gap. FT3168 uses the compatible managed FT5x06 driver. No I/O expander is used. On ESP32-S3, the pinned renderer aligns dirty regions to 2×2 pixels before rasterization. Three internal DMA-capable buffers, each at most native-panel-width × 10 RGB565 pixels, accumulate interleaved dirty rectangles. The renderer outputs panel byte order directly. Rasterization overlaps the preceding DMA transfer in a different buffer. Narrow spans pack additional even rows into the existing byte capacity. Each transfer has a one-second completion timeout; the buffer is not reused until its ISR completion semaphore is received. Brightness and rendering execute on the same task. This S3 path uses no full-screen framebuffer or PSRAM rasterization. On ESP32-P4, Slint renders into a full-screen PSRAM canvas. PPA rotates/copies completed regions into the inactive native DSI framebuffer; a pair of scanout buffers separates rendering from display refresh. Cache synchronization and refresh completion protect buffer reuse. See P4 hardware and memory details . NVS stores display preferences, the selected BLE address and Wi-Fi credentials. BLE forget clears its peer; Wi-Fi forget clears the saved network. Credentials use the ordinary ESP-IDF NVS store; bonding, encrypted credentials and remote control services are not implemented. Runtime orientation Settings cycles through 0°, 90°, 180° and 270° while running, and the preference is saved. Application requests wait until the current input dispatch and frame presentation finish. Logical dimensions swap at 90° and 270°; the S3 software renderer or P4 PPA applies the selected rotation, and touch uses its inverse transform. Panel transfers and touch-controller configuration retain native coordinates. For native dimensions W × H, touch uses these native-to-logical mappings: Rotation Logical position from native (x, y) 0° (x, y) 90° (y, W − 1 − x) 180° (W − 1 − x, H − 1 − y) 270° (H − 1 − y, x) Host tests exhaust every pixel for all four rotations at both 410 × 502 and 480 × 800, and compare touch mapping against actual Slint-rendered fixtures. Changing orientation reuses the target's existing panel buffers. Hardware profiles and build metadata Hardware environments inherit common, via independent hardware profiles in platformio.ini. HOR_RES, VER_RES, I2C/QSPI pins, driver selection, direct reset pins and display enable are profile capabilities. Firmware version is defined once by common.custom_firmware_version, with a release-tag override for CI. HW_TYPE is the environment name, which also determines the release asset filename and board-specific application project identity. Wi-Fi and OTA A maintenance worker ow"}, {"title": "Performance", "url": "performance/", "description": "Configuration decisions and the limits of measured results.", "text": " This records the selected configuration and local measurements for the 2.06-inch display. Startup work timings and memory samples do not establish a sustained frame rate. Setting Selected value Reason CPU 240 MHz ESP32-S3 performance clock UI task stack 32 KiB internal SRAM Keep layout/rendering headroom and permit flash/NVS operations with caches disabled C/C++ optimization Performance ( -O2 ) Retain assertions and checks Flash QIO, 80 MHz, 32 MB Supported target configuration; verify cold boot on this board PSRAM Octal, 80 MHz Board has 8 MB; avoid experimental clock settings Instruction fetch from PSRAM Enabled Execute application instructions from external RAM Read-only data / full XiP helper Disabled Preserve external heap instead of duplicating read-only data Instruction cache 32 KB, 32-byte lines Supported maximum; 64-byte instruction lines do not exist here Data cache 32 KB, 64-byte lines Retain internal heap rather than enlarging cache to 64 KB Always-internal allocation threshold 512 bytes Larger ordinary allocations prefer PSRAM Internal reserve 32 KB Leave internal-only allocations available NimBLE host allocations External PSRAM Preserve internal RAM for controller, driver and task requirements NVS page/key cache PSRAM Persistent bond/settings lookups must not consume DMA/stack headroom Large transport state Explicit PSRAM allocations Keep reassembly, decoded-packet rings and controller discovery outside internal heap Wi-Fi/LwIP allocation preference PSRAM preferred CONFIG_SPIRAM_TRY_ALLOCATE_WIFI_LWIP Wi-Fi DMA/cache pools RX 4, TX 8, TX cache 16 Explicit limits; PSRAM mode otherwise defaults to 16 RX + 16 TX DMA buffers Wi-Fi RX block-ack window 4 Keep the window within the four static RX buffers, per IDF Kconfig guidance TLS heap External PSRAM TLS buffers must not exhaust internal DMA/task memory PSRAM test, assertions, watchdogs Retained No check is disabled for speed Slint mcu-v1.19.5 Hardened bitmap/geometry caches and direct scanline rendering Panel QSPI 80 MHz Hardware-profile clock supplied by DISP_QSPI_HZ scripts/check_sdkconfig.py checks the generated configuration so silent Kconfig fallback is detected. ESP-IDF's derived image/header flash mode can say dio while the bootloader's selected CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_QIO=y enables QIO after initial boot; audit the choice symbol as well as the runtime boot log. The UI embeds bitmap Roboto fonts, limiting large-font glyphs to numeric characters. The header displays connection status beside the Settings cog. On ESP32-S3, dirty regions and three internal scanline buffers avoid full-framebuffer rasterization into PSRAM. The backend now incorporates the applicable transfer changes described below: Each dirty rectangle accumulates independently, avoiding a flush for every interleaved scanline. Narrow dirty spans use the full existing byte allocation, with an even row capacity calculated from span width. Full-width transfers retain ten rows; no additional DMA memory is allocated. A submitted color transfer overlaps rasterization into another buffer. Buffer reuse, address-window commands, brightness, rotation and frame completion wait for DMA ownership to return, with a bounded timeout. An uncompleted transfer is a fatal error rather than permission to overwrite its buffer. Rendering and transfer waits count towards the event-loop budget. Active touch/animation targets approximately 60 Hz; idle input polling retains a 10 ms budget. Every iteration still yields at least one FreeRTOS tick, preserving idle-task watchdog service. Telemetry polling remains 5 Hz and UI data projection 10 Hz. Held contacts dispatch pointer moves only when coordinates change. About > Diagnostics exposes rendered frame count, exponentially averaged frame work and DMA wait, maximum frame work, and average panel transactions. These are work timings, not a delivered FPS measurement. Measure delivered frames over wall-clock time during the same scrolling workload. The portable scanline test exercises the production accumulator with 2,000 randomized interleaved-region cases and native-size cases, checking exact pixel coverage, even windows, allocation canaries, delayed-DMA buffer immutability and batching. AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer are enabled in the Linux host run. The hardware profile supplies the 80 MHz panel clock. This halves theoretical full-screen wire time versus the driver's 40 MHz reference default (about 10.3 ms versus 20.6 ms for 410x502 RGB565, excluding command overhead). It does not by itself double delivered FPS. Address-window caching is not implemented. Existing QIO80 flash, octal80 PSRAM, instruction fetch from PSRAM and pinned renderer caches are already enabled; read-only data and full XiP remain disabled. The connected board has passed cold-boot memory initialization, instruction copying and the PSRAM test, and the user confirmed its dashboard and touch. Radio-load scrolling, corner alignment, extended heap/stack minima and a matched before/after delivered-FPS benchmark still require hardware validation. Large task-only BLE packet queue payloads are explicitly allocated in PSRAM with internal FreeRTOS control blocks. Dynamic FreeRTOS queue creation otherwise consumes internal RAM despite the ordinary malloc threshold; with both BLE roles enabled, that exhausted space for the 12 KiB maintenance-task stack. DMA buffers and all task stacks retain internal allocations. A quiet landscape boot with Wi-Fi PSRAM enabled but unrestricted IDF buffer defaults retained only about 1.1 KiB internal free. A 128-byte malloc-threshold trial did not improve that baseline, so the 512-byte setting is retained. The cause was IDF raising the static Wi-Fi pools to 16 RX and 16 TX buffers when PSRAM mode was enabled. Explicit RX4/TX8/cache16 limits avoid that hidden increase. TLS heap allocation also explicitly uses PSRAM. With RX4/TX8/cache16, TLS/NimBLE external heaps and the original 512-byte threshold, the final quiet boot retained 30,203 bytes internal free and a 15,872-byte largest block, compared with 1,139 bytes free in the earlier unrestricted-pool quiet boot. Both captures used the saved landscape layout. This establishes startup headroom, not an extended radio/TLS soak or FPS comparison. With persistent SMP bonds enabled, the first integrated transport build had only 6,383 bytes internal free at UI acceptance. Explicit PSRAM state allocation plus CONFIG_NVS_ALLOCATE_CACHE_IN_SPIRAM=y raised this to 19,147 bytes (largest block 7,424 bytes) in the final 24-second startup capture. The general 512-byte allocation threshold, internal task stacks, three DMA scanline buffers and 80 MHz panel clock remain unchanged. That earlier 16 KiB UI stack retained only 1,640 bytes at its startup high-water mark. The current UI task uses a 32 KiB internal stack; this capacity is audited in the generated SDK configuration. Startup and all-screen high-water measurements must be repeated after layout changes. NVS operations a"}, {"title": "Planned work", "url": "todo/", "description": "Implementation and hardware acceptance tasks for contributors.", "text": " Controller and phone acceptance [ ] Test WFB and wheel-e discovery and telemetry against the intended controller firmware; compare speed, voltage and angle with the phone app. [ ] Confirm the inferred WFB telemetry tail and live command encodings, including LOCK (12), against hardware. Check lock/unlock acknowledgement and persistence across power cycles. [ ] Exercise encrypted VESC Express pairing, failed PINs, retry and re-pairing after an upstream PIN change. [ ] Check phone proxy interoperability, MTU differences, sustained traffic, lease release and upstream loss over BLE, CAN and UART. [ ] Confirm proxy bond revocation after bike PIN changes/removal, PIN persistence, boot unlock and locked-screen timeout/wake behavior. [ ] Commission CAN/UART wiring with the required transceiver, controller discovery and multiple-controller routing. Device and release acceptance [ ] Soak touch, scrolling and all four orientations with concurrent radio traffic; investigate intermittent idle touch I2C NACKs. [ ] On the GUITION P4 board, verify DSI scanout, PPA rotation, PSRAM cache synchronization and framebuffer handoff under sustained load; check all four GT911 touch mappings and inspect logs for underruns or tearing. [ ] Provision compatible C6 ESP-Hosted firmware, then test Wi-Fi, encrypted upstream BLE, phone proxy and OTA together on the P4/C6 hardware. [ ] Measure sustained display responsiveness, internal heap minima and task-stack headroom under realistic load. [ ] Exercise USB and battery cold boots, reconnect/stale-data behavior and preference persistence. [ ] Test Wi-Fi/OTA success, interruption, cancellation, rollback and first-boot acceptance on the device. [ ] Install each supported target through the browser USB installer on its actual board; check chip/revision selection, complete factory layout, boot, touch and documented settings-reset behavior. Keep the P4 C6 provisioning check separate from the P4 application install. [ ] Confirm the GitHub Pages deployment and generated screenshots on the default branch. Protocol coverage [ ] Resolve signature, argument order and scaling uncertainties in the supplied protocol notes before enabling persisted tuning or additional feature/horn commands. "}, {"title": "Vehicle integrations", "url": "integrations/", "description": "Capabilities and packet formats for WFB, wheel-e and VESC.", "text": " VehicleIntegration is a portable interface with separate implementations for WFB, wheel-e and generic VESC. It owns package polling, telemetry decoding, command encoding, the brand name and supported-feature flags. BLE framing, transport, preferences and display rendering remain shared. The UI and command callbacks both gate unsupported features. Integration Package Dashboard Ride presets / speed limit Bike lock Wheelie Fun Bike 102 VESC setup plus WFB compact telemetry Supported LOCK 12; firmware compatibility needs hardware verification wheel-e 101 VESC setup plus REALTIME_DATA 33 Not exposed; use VESC Tool LOCK 12 VESC None Standard VESC setup telemetry Not exposed Not exposed; local screen PIN still works Choose Bike > Vehicle integration before selecting a device. Auto is the default and probes for both WFB and wheel-e packages on discovered motor controllers. A valid package reply determines the integration; BLE names only affect discovery ranking. Auto falls back to read-only generic VESC only after the supported package probes find no match. Explicit WFB or wheel-e selections still require a matching package reply. Forget the connected bike to select another integration; the decoder and pending writes are invalidated with the session. The BLE peer and selected policy are persisted together after UART notification subscription succeeds. Auto remains Auto after discovery and probes again on reconnect; the detected integration does not silently replace the selected policy. A saved BLE association proves the UART link, not package compatibility; failed package probes cannot enable custom features. Matching BLE names rank ahead of other UART candidates, rather than excluding them. The header, window title and About page use the selected vehicle brand. WFB retains its supplied artwork; wheel-e and generic VESC use text wordmarks. Wheel-e does not display or send WFB's proprietary commands 100/103. wheel-e protocol reference See the dedicated wheel-e protocol notes for identity verification, byte layouts, state flags, locking and compatibility limits. Implemented against buestad/wheel-e at 69382e6 , specifically command handlers , REALTIME_DATA and state definitions . The application payload is [36, 101, command, ...] inside the ordinary VESC frame. Interface 101 is shared with Float/Refloat-family packages, so discovery first requires a complete INFO version-2 reply naming Wheel-E before verifying realtime telemetry. Other interface-101 packages do not receive wheel-e branding or write features. Poll 33 requests float32 values with mask1 0x000a0002 (state flags, pitch, roll), mask2 zero. Responses must echo the exact requested format and masks and contain the complete body; invalid or truncated packets leave telemetry unchanged. Standard command 47 supplies speed, current, battery, temperatures and controller fault. Raw wheel-e states differ from the WFB compatibility states. DISABLED maps to display state 15, STARTUP to 11, READY to 0, RUNNING to 1, THROTTLE to 16 and CRUISE to 17. Charging and fatal-error flags prevent locking. THROTTLE may be locked only with fresh stationary telemetry, zero controller fault and motor current below 2 A; RUNNING and CRUISE are rejected. LOCK uses [36, 101, 12, 1/0] . No WFB configuration is sent to package 101. Protocol fixtures cover both package IDs, masks, endianness, state normalization, truncated replies and unsupported command rejection. A real wheel-e vehicle and phone are still required to verify radio interoperability, telemetry values and lock behavior. "}, {"title": "Locking internals", "url": "locking/", "description": "State confirmation, local PIN storage and timeout policy.", "text": " The Bike page provides six-digit PIN setup, change, removal, local screen locking, bike locking where supported, and a locked-screen timeout of 15/30/60/120 seconds (30 by default). New PINs require confirmation; changing or removing one requires the existing code. There is no default PIN. Keep the chosen code available: no PIN recovery or bypass UI is implemented. The security NVS namespace holds a versioned record containing a random 16-byte salt, SHA-256 PIN digest, lock intent, timeout and failed-attempt count. Plaintext PINs are not stored or logged. The numeric BLE proxy passkey is held in RAM after PIN setup or a successful local unlock; boot with a configured code requires local unlock before the secured proxy is available. Five incorrect entries impose a 30-second cooldown; reboot preserves the failure threshold and starts a fresh cooldown. Read errors or malformed records fail closed. Storage operations must succeed before PIN changes or an explicit bike-lock request can proceed. Once a PIN exists, boot requires that code to access the display. This local screen lock does not itself send a bike command. An explicit bike lock persists intent before queuing LOCK. Bike lock/unlock requests require fresh stationary telemetry, a supported stopped state, zero controller fault, low motor current, a ready connection and exclusive access without a phone proxy lease. The bridge pauses while the local screen is locked. An observed disabled bike also locks the display when a PIN is configured. LOCK (12) sends an explicit boolean state, not a toggle. wheel-e uses package 101. WFB uses package 102 based on its documented command enum and Float-family handler semantics; this WFB behavior has not been verified against the vendor firmware. The UI waits for telemetry from the same connection after the command, including a settling interval. Only DISABLED confirms locking; a stopped non-disabled sample confirms unlocking. Disconnection, stale samples or a five-second timeout leave bike status unconfirmed and the PIN screen in place. The user can retry locking; unlock requires another PIN entry. No automatic command replay occurs across reconnects. After the locked-screen idle timeout, brightness is set to zero. A valid contact wakes the display to the user's saved brightness and is swallowed until release. Partial PIN input is cleared on sleep/wake. Failed touch reads do not manufacture a release. Both PIN and sleep overlays consume input above the underlying UI, and active gestures are cancelled when the PIN overlay opens. The same code controls authenticated pairing to this display's BLE proxy. A change/removal revokes old phone bonds and disconnects old security sessions; it does not alter the upstream device's independent PIN/bond. This PIN protects this display's controls and proxy. It is not a native bike PIN or a hardware immobilizer: another client talking directly to the bike can issue its own commands, and physical flash access is outside this local access-control mechanism. Validate actual propulsion-disable behavior on each supported firmware before relying on the bike lock. "}, {"title": "Bluetooth internals", "url": "bluetooth/", "description": "Connection generations, proxy ownership and transport behavior.", "text": " A fresh display shows Disconnected while its background scan fills the Bike picker. Scanning without a previously verified bike does not imply a reconnect attempt. Selecting a bike shows Connecting, followed by VESC service discovery and notification subscription. Connected means the Nordic UART Service write and notify characteristics have been found and its notification CCCD write succeeded; telemetry freshness is tracked separately by the application. The selected candidate remains in memory during bounded retry attempts. Its address is saved to the ble NVS namespace only after notification subscription succeeds. A peer_ok marker records that verification. An older address without that marker can still be tried, but it cannot produce the remembered-peer reconnect state until it has been verified. Forget clears the saved address and marker, invalidates the current stream session and returns to Disconnected. Saved bike association and Bluetooth SMP bonding serve different purposes. The verified association stores the selected BLE address and vehicle integration; the SMP bond stores connection security keys. A secured upstream device has a separate six-digit pairing prompt. The upstream PIN itself is not saved or reused by the proxy. Persistent bonds allow reconnection without inventing a default code; authentication changes require pairing again. Controller-specific behavior, changed credentials and private-address identity resolution still require hardware acceptance testing. Asynchronous scan, connect, MTU, GATT discovery and subscription callbacks carry a generation token. A callback from an earlier attempt cannot promote a newer attempt to Connected. Notifications additionally require the current central connection handle, expected UART TX characteristic handle and ready state. Every disconnected, forgotten or paused stream invalidates queued telemetry and writes through its session identifier. OTA pause is displayed explicitly and prevents scanning or connecting until resumed. The portable lifecycle tests cover fresh repeated scans, first-connect/discovery failures, successful subscription, restoration, disconnect/retry, late callbacks, pause/resume, unsupported authentication and Forget. Hardware testing with actual VESC controllers remains necessary for advertising variants, connection loss, MTU negotiation and security requirements. Controllers using rotating private addresses require identity resolution beyond a saved over-the-air address. Optional VESC BLE proxy The BLE proxy setting is off by default. The display advertises its own user-editable name, initially WB Disp , with the standard Nordic UART Service. This name is independent of the upstream BLE device, selected integration and discovered main controller. BLE and UART provide an ordinary VESC byte stream. CAN exposes a generic adapter with firmware discovery, CAN ping and explicit CAN forwarding; the phone supplies its own controller IDs. The proxy does not consult or rewrite the display client's main-controller route. The proxy's PIN requirement comes only from the local bike lock PIN. Without a bike PIN it has no PIN requirement. With one, phone pairing requires that six-digit code and authenticated encryption. Changing or removing the bike PIN revokes old phone security sessions and bonds; upstream bonds remain independent. A configured bike PIN requires local unlock after boot before its numeric proxy passkey is available in RAM. A connected phone owns the UART conversation, including before it enables notifications. VESC replies have no request identifier, so the display suspends its own polling, configuration writes and firmware maintenance for that lease. A display packet already in transmission finishes before phone data begins; queued display requests are discarded. Main-controller discovery also waits while the phone owns the conversation. The dashboard passively displays recognized telemetry replies from the phone conversation. If the app does not request the setup/package messages the selected integration recognizes, readings become stale rather than inventing measurements or injecting competing polls. The BLE/UART bridge forwards raw bytes in order without imposing the dashboard decoder's 1024-byte packet limit. Native CAN necessarily terminates serial framing and uses the bounded VESC CAN packet codec before reframing replies for the phone. Phone and VESC MTUs may differ: upstream notifications are split into phone-sized notifications while retaining unsent bytes. Both directions have bounded queues and bounded allocation/retry waits. Proxy queue overflow or an unrecoverable send failure terminates the phone conversation and resets the upstream link instead of silently dropping bytes. A slow passive dashboard decoder can be reset independently without truncating a phone bulk transfer. Session/lease epochs reject queued bytes from previous connections. Disabling the setting, a lost VESC connection, host reset or transfer failure ends the phone lease. After a phone leaves, the central link is reset before normal polling resumes so partial app packets and delayed replies cannot blend into a new display conversation. Display OTA reserves the proxy admission lock before starting; a phone already owning the VESC blocks OTA, while a later phone cannot acquire the reserved link. The proxy provides the phone the VESC access normally available over its BLE dongle, including configuration and firmware commands. Enable it only when that access is wanted. Its pairing requirement follows the local bike PIN policy; this is separate from upstream BLE credentials. Hardware validation with a real VESC and phone app is required for discovery, both MTUs, sustained transfers, disconnects and app firmware-update behavior. Portable tests verify the ownership, default-off, maintenance and stale-stream policy, not radio interoperability. Discovery prioritizes names containing WFB or wheel-e (case insensitive), while other NUS devices, VESC names and the saved peer remain selectable. Known scan candidates can receive name updates from separate scan-response packets. A matching device can displace a generic candidate when the six-entry cache is full. The UI sorts a snapshot; selection resolves the displayed BLE address under the central mutex rather than using a potentially reordered scan index. The verified association stores the BLE address, selected integration policy and last connected name in one versioned NVS blob. Auto is the default and remains selected after discovery. It probes WFB and wheel-e packages on discovered motor controllers, then selects the first verified match. The probes run again on reconnect, including for renamed devices. Names only rank discovery candidates. When neither supported package is found, Auto can use read-only generic VESC telemetry. Mismatched custom replies are rejected and cannot enable writes. The proxy advertises when its selected physical interface is ready, independ"}, {"title": "Transport internals", "url": "transports/", "description": "Physical interfaces, native CAN framing and controller discovery.", "text": " Vehicle integrations exchange complete VESC application payloads through transport::Interface . BLE, CAN and UART implementations own their wire framing, input buffering and connection generations. Vehicle protocol selection is independent of the physical interface. The shared implementation lives under firmware/components/bike/ ; hardware drivers and transport ownership live under firmware/main/ . BLE is the default. Settings → Vehicle connection cycles through interfaces available in the target profile. The selection is saved in NVS. An unavailable or invalid saved interface falls back to BLE. A switch invalidates telemetry, controller discovery and queued writes; it does not replay a pending setting change. OTA pauses the selected interface. A phone proxy lease blocks changes to its active upstream. Configuring a target Add definitions to that target's build_flags in platformio.ini . Both pins in a pair are required. Pins are deliberately absent from the supplied Waveshare 2.06 profile until the wiring is chosen; they are not inferred from the display size or assigned to arbitrary GPIOs. Flag Meaning Default CAN_TX , CAN_RX ESP32 TWAI signal pins Not configured; CAN unavailable CAN_BITRATE CAN bit rate 500000; also supports 125000, 250000 and 1000000 CAN_LOCAL_ID This display/adapter's CAN address 254; choose an unused ID from 0–254 UART_TX , UART_RX Vehicle serial pins Not configured; UART unavailable BIKE_UART_PORT ESP32 UART peripheral 1; supports 1 or 2, reserving UART0 for the console UART_BAUD Serial baud rate 115200; allowed range 9600–1000000 Use GPIO numbers appropriate for the actual board and free of its display/touch/storage connections. CAN requires an external compatible transceiver, common reference and correctly terminated bus; ESP32 TWAI pins are not CAN-H/CAN-L. UART uses compatible logic-level TX/RX, crossed between endpoints, a common reference and matching VESC serial configuration. It is 8N1 without hardware flow control. Framing and ownership BLE and UART use ordinary VESC length/CRC serial framing. Native CAN uses extended frames with FILL_RX_BUFFER (5), FILL_RX_BUFFER_LONG (6), PROCESS_RX_BUFFER (7) and PROCESS_SHORT_BUFFER (8). Application UART frames are not sent as CAN payloads. The native receive path checks destination, sender, reply mode, contiguous offsets, bounds, assembly timeout and payload CRC before delivering a packet. The wired driver runs in a separate task with bounded queues and cancellable writes. CAN uses ESP-IDF 6.1's handle-based esp_twai node API. Eight persistent transmit slots retain each frame and its bytes until the completion callback returns ownership; a 64-frame internal queue copies received frames out of the ISR. Callbacks do not run vehicle protocols, allocate memory or block. This internal storage is allocated only while CAN is open. Transmit queue waits are bounded to 20 ms; a frame still pending after 250 ms triggers a session retry. Receive overflow, transmit failure and bus-off also restart the session rather than continuing with an incomplete packet stream. Packet storage and assembly buffers use the configured external-memory allocation policy; queue controls and task stacks remain internal. Driver failures, CAN bus-off, transport changes and reconnects invalidate the session. Late packets and writes from previous sessions are discarded. UART framing, native CAN framing and the phone's raw stream have separate parser state. Finding the main controller The display performs read-only discovery before normal polling or configuration writes: On BLE/UART, request COMM_PING_CAN (62), including the directly attached controller as a candidate. Native CAN sweeps controller IDs with CAN ping (17) and collects pong (18) responses addressed to this display. Query COMM_FW_VERSION (0) for candidates. Serial forwarding uses COMM_FORWARD_CAN (34); native CAN addresses the candidate directly. Exclude non-motor hardware such as BMS/custom adapters, then verify the selected vehicle integration with its telemetry request. Custom configuration metadata and a successful package reply identify installed package support. Use the first compatible controller with a custom package/configuration. There is no multiple-controller choice prompt. Explicit generic VESC mode may fall back to a plain motor controller for read-only standard telemetry when no package controller is found. Firmware identity parsing is bounded and handles the hardware name, UUID, version, hardware type and custom-configuration count. Queries are serialized, with quiet/settling periods because serial CAN-forwarded replies do not carry a request/source identifier. Native CAN replies retain their source ID. Bounded discovery retries handle missing devices; reconnects discard the previous discovery result. Bike → Find main controller again restarts discovery. The Bike page reports the selected controller and firmware. Transparent phone BLE proxy The optional proxy is a separate VESC client path. It does not use the display's main-controller route or vehicle integration to rewrite the phone's commands. The phone owns the upstream while connected; display polling, discovery and configuration writes pause. After the phone leaves, the upstream/parser session is reset before the display resumes. Vehicle connection Phone proxy behavior BLE Raw ordered byte stream to the connected BLE UART peer UART Raw ordered byte stream to the configured serial port CAN Generic adapter endpoint: firmware identity, CAN discovery and explicit CAN forwarding On CAN, a direct firmware query identifies a custom adapter, not a motor controller. COMM_PING_CAN lists responding nodes. COMM_FORWARD_CAN sends the phone's payload to its explicitly requested CAN ID and returns the unmodified VESC reply inside serial framing. A direct motor command without a CAN destination is not implicitly routed to the display's selected bike; the adapter tells the app to select a CAN controller. Native CAN packet size limits and CRC framing apply. The phone app performs its own controller discovery/selection, as with a CAN-capable VESC adapter. Settings → BLE name controls this display's advertised/GAP name, default WB Disp , independently of the upstream and branding. Names are saved in NVS and limited to 29 UTF-8 bytes so the complete name fits a legacy scan response. A connected phone may refresh its cached name only after reconnecting. The bike lock PIN controls the proxy's pairing requirement; the upstream BLE PIN is independent. See Bluetooth and PINs and locking . References and verification Native packet formats and firmware discovery were checked against VESC firmware 21e66bb , particularly CAN transport and command responses . Hardware uses the ESP-IDF TWAI driver . Host tests exercise CAN boundaries and CRCs, missing/reordered/expired fragments, wrong destinations/senders, first-package discovery, BMS exclusion, native bridge commands and PIN-policy changes. Driver ownership tests cover pending tr"}, {"title": "wheel-e protocol notes", "url": "reference/wheel-e-protocol/", "description": "Package identity, modern telemetry, locking and compatibility for wheel-e.", "text": " This document describes the wheel-e contract implemented by Wheelie Fun Display. The reference is buestad/wheel-e commit 69382e6 , whose package name is Wheel-E . Protocol compatibility is established with replies from the connected controller, independently of its Bluetooth name. Supported contract Operation VESC/application command Display behavior Motor-controller identification COMM_FW_VERSION ( 0 ) Requires a valid controller firmware identity; cached routes must also match the saved UUID. Package identification COMM_CUSTOM_APP_DATA ( 36 ), interface 101 , INFO ( 0 ), version 2 Requires a complete reply identifying Wheel-E . Standard motor telemetry COMM_GET_VALUES_SETUP ( 47 ) Supplies speed, battery, voltage, currents, temperatures and controller fault. Package telemetry Interface 101 , REALTIME_DATA ( 33 ) Requires the exact float32/state/pitch/roll request described below. Bike lock/unlock Interface 101 , LOCK ( 12 ) Sends an explicit boolean and confirms the resulting state through fresh telemetry. Tuning, configuration, lights and remote throttle Other wheel-e commands Not exposed or transmitted by this display. Use VESC Tool for those features. The implementation lives in the integration , controller discovery and lock policy . These notes distinguish the upstream protocol from the display's deliberately smaller feature set. Transport and envelope The byte sequences below are complete VESC payloads , before serial length/CRC framing or native CAN fragmentation. They can travel through the shared BLE, UART or CAN transport. See transport internals for framing, CAN forwarding and controller routing. A wheel-e application payload begins: 24 65 CC ... │ │ └─ wheel-e command, hexadecimal │ └──── interface 101 (0x65) └─────── COMM_CUSTOM_APP_DATA, 36 (0x24) The upstream command pages omit this three-byte prefix when describing command-specific offsets. Every offset in this document includes it. Multi-byte integers use big-endian byte order. Float32 telemetry uses the upstream buffer_append_float32_auto format: IEEE 754 binary32 in big-endian order, with subnormal values flushed to zero by the encoder. Command envelope , numeric encoders . Interface 101 is shared with Float/Refloat-family packages. Its presence, or a compatible telemetry response alone, is not a unique wheel-e identity. The display therefore requires the package-name check below before enabling wheel-e branding and features. Discovery and package identity Auto checks supported packages on discovered motor controllers. A manual wheel-e selection still performs verification. The relevant sequence is: Obtain a valid motor-controller firmware response, excluding non-motor adapters. Request INFO version 2 and verify the package name. Request modern REALTIME_DATA and validate the exact response format. Enable the wheel-e integration only after both package checks succeed. The standard firmware request [0] and the wheel-e application request [36, 101, 0, 2, 0] are different messages. The first identifies VESC firmware; the second identifies the installed package. The display stores bounded NVS route hints to accelerate later connections. A hint changes query order only: firmware UUID, package identity and telemetry are checked again. A missing controller, mismatched identity, removed package or failed probe returns to full discovery. See vehicle integrations for Auto and manual selection behavior. INFO version 2 Request: 24 65 00 02 00 │ └─ flags = 0 └──── INFO response version requested = 2 The referenced handler returns this 61-byte VESC payload: Offset Bytes Field 0 1 36 : VESC custom application data 1 1 101 : package interface 2 1 0 : INFO command 3 1 2 : actual INFO response version 4 1 Request flags echoed; 0 for the display's request 5 20 Package name, zero-padded fixed-width string 25 3 Package major, minor and patch version, one byte each 28 20 Version suffix, zero-padded fixed-width string 48 4 Build Git hash prefix 52 4 System tick rate in Hz 56 4 Package capability flags 60 1 Extra flags The display accepts a case-insensitive exact Wheel-E name with zero padding, version 2 , echoed flags 0 , a nonzero tick rate and the complete layout. Float , Refloat , longer names with a Wheel-E prefix, legacy version-1 replies and truncated replies do not establish wheel-e identity. INFO capability bits are not used to expose additional controls. The reference build's tick rate is 10,000 Hz. Package version strings are diagnostic information; successful version-2 identity and modern telemetry replies are the compatibility requirements. INFO specification , handler , package name . REALTIME_DATA, command 33 The display requests float32 encoding and three fields from mask1: state flags at bit 1, pitch at bit 17 and roll at bit 19. Their combined mask is 0x000A0002 . Mask2 is zero. Request, 12 bytes : 24 65 21 01 00 0A 00 02 00 00 00 00 │ └─────────┘ └─────────┘ │ mask1 mask2 └─ control_flags = 1: float32 Response, 28 bytes : Offset Bytes Field Interpretation 0 3 Command prefix [36, 101, 33] 3 1 Control flags Exactly 1 4 4 Mask1 Exactly 0x000A0002 8 4 Mask2 Exactly 0 12 4 Package time Unsigned ticks; divide by INFO tick rate for seconds 16 4 State flags Packed unsigned integer described below 20 4 Pitch Float32 degrees 24 4 Roll Float32 degrees Selected fields follow mask-bit order. No float16 variant, additional mask fields or legacy compact telemetry layout is accepted by this decoder. The display rejects incorrect headers, flags or masks, incomplete/extended bodies, unknown run states, non-finite angles and angles outside ±180°. Rejected packets leave the last valid sample unchanged. REALTIME_DATA specification , response encoder . State flags Bits Field Display use 29–28 Package mode Not currently projected into the UI 26–24 Run state Normalized using the table below 23–22 Footpad state Not currently projected into the UI 21 Charging Overrides the normalized state to 14 ; blocks locking 20 Fatal error Overrides the normalized state to 255 ; blocks locking 17 Darkride Not currently projected into the UI 16 Wheelslip Not currently projected into the UI 15–12 Setpoint adjustment type Not currently projected into the UI 11–8 Stop condition Not currently projected into the UI 7–0 Beep reason Not currently projected into the UI Raw run state Upstream name Display's internal state 0 DISABLED 15 1 STARTUP 11 2 READY 0 3 RUNNING 1 4 THROTTLE 16 5 CRUISE 17 These are modern wheel-e states, not the four-bit legacy Float compatibility states. Keeping THROTTLE and CRUISE distinct allows the lock policy to reject cruise while considering stationary throttle mode. If charging and fatal error are both set, fatal error takes precedence. State definitions , flag encoder . Standard telemetry and freshness After verification, the display polls standard VESC setup telemetry ( 47 ) together with wheel-e realtime data ( 33 ) at 5 Hz. Standard telemetry supplies signed speed in m/s, voltage, battery percentage, motor/battery "}, {"title": "WFB protocol notes", "url": "reference/vesc-protocol/", "description": "VESC transport, telemetry and configuration reference for WFB.", "text": " VESC communications reference for Wheelie app version 1.0.0. Package com.thefloatlife.Wheelie Version 1.0.0 ( versionCode 6, iOS buildNumber 9) Entries marked inferred describe unconfirmed encodings or behavior and require device verification before enabling writes. Later app or controller versions may change the protocol behavior documented here. 1. Package compatibility The app expects a compatible custom package to be installed on the controller; it does not provide a .vescpkg or controller firmware installation path. The app carries the custom-config schema described in §6. It can read and write that configuration without fetching the package, but refuses unsupported signatures with \"Package Version Unsupported or failed to get config\" . There is no supported Lisp source upload path. COMM_LISP_PRINT replies are explicitly discarded. 2. Transport — BLE The GATT profile is the standard VESC one: Constant UUID Role VESC_SERVICE_UUID 6e400001-b5a3-f393-e0a9-e50e24dcca9e Nordic UART Service VESC_CHARACTERISTICS_RX_UUID 6e400002-b5a3-f393-e0a9-e50e24dcca9e write, app -> board VESC_CHARACTERISTICS_TX_UUID 6e400003-b5a3-f393-e0a9-e50e24dcca9e notify, board -> app This is byte-for-byte the NUS profile that nrf51_vesc and VESC Express expose - no custom service, no renamed characteristics, no vendor base UUID. Any tool that speaks stock VESC BLE will connect. All of this app's customisation lives above the transport, inside COMM_CUSTOM_APP_DATA payloads. After discoverAllServicesAndCharacteristicsForDevice the app walks servicesForDevice and pushes 'vesc' into a capability array if the NUS service is present; that tag gates binding the vescMessageHandler listener. Device discovery filters on advertised name — helper bleDefaultNameIncludesWfb . Write path — writeBuffer(deviceId, packet) chunkSize = manager.mtu - 3 for each chunk: writeCharacteristicWithoutResponseForDevice( deviceId, VESC_SERVICE_UUID, VESC_CHARACTERISTICS_RX_UUID, chunk.toString('base64')) await Promise.all(all chunk promises) Note the chunks are issued concurrently and awaited together, not sequentially. Ordering depends on the platform BLE queue preserving submission order; on a stack that reorders, a multi-chunk packet would arrive scrambled. Android permissions requested: BLUETOOTH , BLUETOOTH_ADMIN , BLUETOOTH_SCAN , BLUETOOTH_CONNECT , ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION , ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION , FOREGROUND_SERVICE , FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE , WAKE_LOCK . iOS declares the bluetooth-central background mode. There is a VescBackgroundService that keeps the connection alive under a foreground service. 3. Framing — generatePacket(payload, canId?) Standard VESC serial framing: short (len <= 256): 0x02 len:u8 payload crc16:u16be 0x03 long (len > 256): 0x03 len:u16be payload crc16:u16be 0x03 CAN forwarding is applied at frame-build time: if canId is supplied and payload[0] !== COMM_FORWARD_CAN , the two bytes [34, canId] are prepended. The send path ( writeData ) does the same automatically from _primaryCANDeviceId , so a caller normally doesn't pass canId explicitly. CRC CRC-16/CCITT (XMODEM), init 0x0000 , table-driven, byte-at-a-time: crc = table[((crc >> 8) ^ byte) & 0xFF] ^ (crc << 8) // truncated to u16 Matches crc.c in vedderb/bldc . Two framing bugs worth knowing about Off-by-one on the length-form boundary. The branch is len <= 256 for the short form; upstream uses len <= 255 . A payload of exactly 256 bytes is emitted with a single length byte of 256 & 0xFF = 0 , which the firmware will read as a zero-length packet. Payloads of exactly 256 bytes are therefore unusable. No 3-byte length form. Upstream supports start byte 0x04 with a 24-bit length for payloads > 65535. Not implemented here — irrelevant for the commands this app sends, but a limit if you're reusing the code. VescBuffer A reimplementation of VESC's buffer.c . Readers: readUInt8 , readInt8 , readUInt16 , readInt16 , readUInt32 , readInt32 , readDouble16(scale) , readDouble32(scale) , readFloat32 , readDouble32Auto , readString , slice , size , raw . Writers mirror these, plus writeDouble32_Old retained for compatibility. readDouble16(scale) / readDouble32(scale) are the scaled-integer forms (read a signed int, divide by scale). readDouble32Auto is the float32-packed-in-uint32 form. 4. Packet ID enum 127 COMM_* IDs are defined. IDs 0–90 and 92–101 match upstream bldc/datatypes.h exactly. Beyond that this app targets a fork and the numbering diverges. Full table: ID Name ID Name 0 COMM_FW_VERSION 51 COMM_GET_VALUES_SETUP_SELECTIVE 1 COMM_JUMP_TO_BOOTLOADER 52 COMM_EXT_NRF_PRESENT 2 COMM_ERASE_NEW_APP 53 COMM_EXT_NRF_ESB_SET_CH_ADDR 3 COMM_WRITE_NEW_APP_DATA 54 COMM_EXT_NRF_ESB_SEND_DATA 4 COMM_GET_VALUES 55 COMM_EXT_NRF_ESB_RX_DATA 5 COMM_SET_DUTY 56 COMM_EXT_NRF_SET_ENABLED 6 COMM_SET_CURRENT 57 COMM_DETECT_MOTOR_FLUX_LINKAGE_OPENLOOP 7 COMM_SET_CURRENT_BRAKE 58 COMM_DETECT_APPLY_ALL_FOC 8 COMM_SET_RPM 59 COMM_JUMP_TO_BOOTLOADER_ALL_CAN 9 COMM_SET_POS 60 COMM_ERASE_NEW_APP_ALL_CAN 10 COMM_SET_HANDBRAKE 61 COMM_WRITE_NEW_APP_DATA_ALL_CAN 11 COMM_SET_DETECT 62 COMM_PING_CAN 12 COMM_SET_SERVO_POS 63 COMM_APP_DISABLE_OUTPUT 13 COMM_SET_MCCONF 64 COMM_TERMINAL_CMD_SYNC 14 COMM_GET_MCCONF 65 COMM_GET_IMU_DATA 15 COMM_GET_MCCONF_DEFAULT 66 COMM_BM_CONNECT 16 COMM_SET_APPCONF 67 COMM_BM_ERASE_FLASH_ALL 17 COMM_GET_APPCONF 68 COMM_BM_WRITE_FLASH 18 COMM_GET_APPCONF_DEFAULT 69 COMM_BM_REBOOT 19 COMM_SAMPLE_PRINT 70 COMM_BM_DISCONNECT 20 COMM_TERMINAL_CMD 71 COMM_BM_MAP_PINS_DEFAULT 21 COMM_PRINT 72 COMM_BM_MAP_PINS_NRF5X 22 COMM_ROTOR_POSITION 73 COMM_ERASE_BOOTLOADER 23 COMM_EXPERIMENT_SAMPLE 74 COMM_ERASE_BOOTLOADER_ALL_CAN 24 COMM_DETECT_MOTOR_PARAM 75 COMM_PLOT_INIT 25 COMM_DETECT_MOTOR_R_L 76 COMM_PLOT_DATA 26 COMM_DETECT_MOTOR_FLUX_LINKAGE 77 COMM_PLOT_ADD_GRAPH 27 COMM_DETECT_ENCODER 78 COMM_PLOT_SET_GRAPH 28 COMM_DETECT_HALL_FOC 79 COMM_GET_DECODED_BALANCE 29 COMM_REBOOT 80 COMM_BM_MEM_READ 30 COMM_ALIVE 81 COMM_WRITE_NEW_APP_DATA_LZO 31 COMM_GET_DECODED_PPM 82 COMM_WRITE_NEW_APP_DATA_ALL_CAN_LZO 32 COMM_GET_DECODED_ADC 83 COMM_BM_WRITE_FLASH_LZO 33 COMM_GET_DECODED_CHUK 84 COMM_SET_CURRENT_REL 34 COMM_FORWARD_CAN 85 COMM_CAN_FWD_FRAME 35 COMM_SET_CHUCK_DATA 86 COMM_SET_BATTERY_CUT 36 COMM_CUSTOM_APP_DATA 87 COMM_SET_BLE_NAME 37 COMM_NRF_START_PAIRING 88 COMM_SET_BLE_PIN 38 COMM_GPD_SET_FSW 89 COMM_SET_CAN_MODE 39 COMM_GPD_BUFFER_NOTIFY 90 COMM_GET_IMU_CALIBRATION 40 COMM_GPD_BUFFER_SIZE_LEFT 92 COMM_GET_CUSTOM_CONFIG_XML 41 COMM_GPD_FILL_BUFFER 93 COMM_GET_CUSTOM_CONFIG 42 COMM_GPD_OUTPUT_SAMPLE 94 COMM_GET_CUSTOM_CONFIG_DEFAULT 43 COMM_GPD_SET_MODE 95 COMM_SET_CUSTOM_CONFIG 44 COMM_GPD_FILL_BUFFER_INT8 96 COMM_BMS_GET_VALUES 45 COMM_GPD_FILL_BUFFER_INT16 97 COMM_BMS_SET_CHARGE_ALLOWED 46 COMM_GPD_SET_BUFFER_INT_SCALE 98 COMM_BMS_SET_BALANCE_OVERRIDE 47 COMM_GET_VALUES_SETUP 99 COMM_BMS_RESET_COUNTERS 48 COMM_SET_MCCONF_TEMP 100 COMM_BMS_FORCE_BALANCE ⚠ 49 COMM_SET_MCCONF_TEMP_SETUP 100 COMM_GET_MCCONF_TEMP ⚠ 50 COMM_GET_VALUES_SELECTIVE 101"}, {"title": "WFB bike layer", "url": "reference/wfb-bike-layer/", "description": "WFB command layouts and configuration details.", "text": " Companion to wheelie-vesc-comms-api.md . Everything here is the vendor-specific layer that sits on top of COMM_CUSTOM_APP_DATA . 1. Frame shape Every bike command is one VESC packet: 0x02 len 36 102 <sub> <payload…> crc16 0x03 │ │ │ │ │ │ │ └── FloatPacketTypes sub-command │ │ └──────── AppPacketTypes.WFB = 102 │ └──────────── COMM_CUSTOM_APP_DATA = 36 └───────────────── length byte getVescAppData reads bytes 0 and 1 of the payload and returns null unless byte 0 is 102 . Stock Float package boards advertise 101, so this app is a closed loop with its own firmware package — it won't read a Float or Refloat board even though it understands the config format. Two send helpers exist: writeData(...bytes) — fire and forget, used by the three configure commands. getData(...bytes) — writes and awaits a matching response, used for polling. Both prepend [COMM_FORWARD_CAN, canId] automatically when _primaryCANDeviceId is set. 2. Sub-command 100 — BIKE_RIDE_CONFIGURE Sets the four motor-response parameters as a 3-byte packed payload . Full frame is 6 bytes of payload: 36 102 100 b0 b1 b2 Built by createRideModeCommand(maxCurrent, maxCurrentBrake, rampAlphaPos, rampAlphaNeg) : Byte Content b0 low nibble clamp(round(maxCurrent / 10), 0, 15) b0 high nibble clamp(round(abs(maxCurrentBrake) / 10), 0, 15) b1 rampAlphaToByte(rampAlphaPos) b2 rampAlphaToByte(rampAlphaNeg) So currents are transmitted in 10 A units, 4 bits each , brake current as an absolute value in the high nibble. Range on the wire is 0–150 A in 10 A steps — which is exactly why max_current in the config table tops out at 150. rampAlphaToByte(a) — piecewise non-linear quantisation A single byte covering four orders of magnitude, with finer resolution at the low (smoother) end: Input range Byte value Resolution a <= 0.0001 0 — 0.0001 < a <= 0.001 round((a − 0.0001) / 0.0001) → 0–9 0.0001 0.001 < a <= 0.01 round(10 + (a − 0.001) / 0.0005) → 10–28 0.0005 0.01 < a <= 0.1 round(28 + (a − 0.01) / 0.001) → 28–118 0.001 0.1 < a <= 0.5 round(118 + (a − 0.1) / 0.005) → 118–198 0.005 0.5 < a < 1 round(198 + (a − 0.5) / 0.01) → 198–248 0.01 a >= 1 248 — The bands join cleanly (0.001→10, 0.01→28, 0.1→118, 0.5→198, 1.0→248), so this is a deliberate companding curve, not accumulated drift. Byte values 249–255 are unreachable. Ride mode presets RIDE_MODE_PARAMS : id maxCurrent maxCurrentBrake rampAlphaPos rampAlphaNeg sport 150 −90 0.015 0.008 cruise 150 −90 0.003 0.008 eco 90 −90 0.002 0.005 custom 150 −90 0.003 0.008 sport and cruise differ only in throttle ramp; braking is identical across all four presets. custom is seeded from cruise . UI metadata: Sport #AC0E0E \"High performance mode with near-instant throttle response\", Cruise #0E77AC \"Smooth, predictable power delivery suitable for most riders\", Eco #198E13 \"Limited acceleration and speed for a more relaxed and efficient ride\", Custom #777777 , plus an unknown pseudo-mode (\"Configuration doesn't match any known mode\"). Custom mode: slider → parameter mapping When the user picks Custom, the four values come from sliders: maxCurrent = 60 + throttleCurrent * 10 maxCurrentBrake = −(60 + brakeCurrent * 10) rampAlphaPos = throttleRamp === 10 ? 1 : RAMP_VALUES[throttleRamp − 1] rampAlphaNeg = brakeRamp === 10 ? 1 : RAMP_VALUES[brakeRamp − 1] RAMP_VALUES = [0.0005, 0.001, 0.002, 0.003, 0.005, 0.008, 0.015, 0.03, 0.06, 0.1] Slider position 10 short-circuits to rampAlpha = 1 (instant response, byte 248) rather than reading RAMP_VALUES[9] = 0.1 . Index 9 of the table is therefore dead — position 9 gives 0.06 and position 10 jumps straight to 1.0. Whether that's intentional (\"10 = instant\") or an off-by-one is unclear from the bytecode; the effect is a large discontinuity at the top of the slider. Reverse mapping — determineInitialRideMode On connect, the app reads the live config and tries to match it back to a preset using isCloseEnough(a, b, epsilon = 0.001) on each of the four values. No match → the unknown pseudo-mode. A match on custom 's values → 'custom' . This is how the UI shows which mode a board is currently in without the firmware reporting a mode ID. Logging is verbose here: \"Sending ride mode command for <name>:\" with the resolved values and commandBytes , then \"Successfully sent ride mode configuration for <name>\" . \"Cannot select unknown mode\" throws if the unknown pseudo-mode is somehow selected. 3. Sub-command 101 — BIKE_WHEELIE_CONFIGURE Defined but not transmitted by app version 1.0.0. A supported payload encoding for this sub-command is not established here. The associated UI exists but is largely disabled. WHEELIE_MODES : id name icon colour disabled agro Agro 3 #AC0E0E true standard Standard 2 #0E77AC false float Float 1 #198E13 true Descriptions: Agro — \"Stiff, locked in response; sticks to set wheelie angle at all costs\"; Standard — \"Secure feeling wheelie with some give for a smooth, fun ride\"; Float — \"Soft, loose wheelie for a more floaty feel\". The mode picker includes selection and cancellation state, but two options are disabled and no transmit path is documented. These controls do not establish a supported sub-command 101 payload. Separate WHEELIE_CONFIG_SIGNATURES and isWheelieConfig identifiers suggest another config profile may have been planned; that remains unconfirmed. Wheelie tuning in this build goes through the ordinary COMM_SET_CUSTOM_CONFIG path instead (the WHEELIE_KP* / MAHONY_KP params in §5), not through sub-command 101. 4. Sub-command 102 — BIKE_FEATURE_CONFIGURE The big one: 13 payload bytes , all scalar, no length prefix. Full frame payload is 16 bytes. 36 102 102 b0 b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6 b7 b8 b9 b10 b11 b12 # Field Encoding Wire range 0 selectedFunction {wheelie:0, turbo:1, horn:2, disabled:3} , unknown → 0, then min(x, 3) 0–3 1 wheelieAngle clamp(round(deg × 2), 10, 110) 10–110 (5–55°) 2 throttleBrakeTilt bool ? 1 : 0 0/1 3 throttleTiltAngle clamp(round((deg + 20) × 2), 0, 80) 0–80 (−20…+20°) 4 brakeTiltAngle clamp(round((deg + 20) × 2), 0, 80) 0–80 (−20…+20°) 5 persistWheelieThrottle bool ? 1 : 0 0/1 6 persistWheelieBrake bool ? 1 : 0 0/1 7 antiLoopEnabled bool ? 1 : 0 0/1 8 sameAngleAsButton bool ? 1 : 0 0/1 9 antiLoopAngle clamp(round(deg × 2), 10, 110) 10–110 (5–55°) 10 wheelLiftResistance clamp(round(x × 10), 0, 30) 0–30 (0–3.0) 11 hornStrength hornStrengthUiToWireByte(ui) , clamped 0–250 10–100 12 hornNoteIndex clamp(round(i), 0, 27) 0–27 Two encoding families worth noting: angles are ×2 with a +20 bias where they can go negative (tilt angles), and plain ×2 where they can't (wheelie and anti-loop angles, both 5–55°). The +20/×2 bias is what lets a signed −20…+20° range fit an unsigned byte. hornStrengthUiToWireByte(ui) = max(10, min(100, round(ui × 10))) — UI 1–10 maps to wire 10–100. The inverse used when reading firmware state is hornStrengthFirmwareToUi(fw) = max(1, min(10, round(fw))) , which does not divide by 10. The two paths disagree by a factor of 10; the horn_strength config field (§5) is a 0–100 double with default 4, and t"}, {"title": "Theme guide", "url": "reference/theme/", "description": "Colours, typography and component styling for the display.", "text": " This guide describes the styles used by the Slint firmware and shared simulator. The implementation lives in ui/theme.slint and the reusable components in ui/ . Colour Use a black background with raised dark cards. Reserve colour for live state, selection, warnings and the speed gauge. Theme token Colour Use bg #000000 Screen and page backgrounds surface #1D1D1D Cards, rows and panels raised #2A2A2A Raised controls and selected surfaces sunken #141414 Recessed or disabled surfaces control #444444 Control borders and value fields track #555555 Gauge and progress tracks accent #69D2DF Selection, active icons and battery fill text #FFFFFF Main text and values secondary #BBBBBB Secondary labels and units muted #AAAAAA Inactive labels dim #808080 Unselected settings cog and subdued details danger #FF4444 Faults and overheating warning #FFC300 Pending or cautionary state gauge #FFD700 Speed arc eco #198E13 Eco preset cruise #0E77AC Standard preset sport #AC0E0E Sport preset The battery bar uses dark text on cyan. Other cards keep light text on dark surfaces. Never use colour alone to communicate a fault or unavailable action. Typography Use the bundled Roboto font with a clear weight hierarchy: Weight Use 500 Body text, supporting values and units 700 Buttons, section labels and the connection header 800 Page headings and prominent numeric values Keep literal font weights in Slint so the embedded compiler includes the required faces. The firmware font plan includes 14, 16, 18, 20, 24, 28, 36, 48, 60, 72 and 84 px bitmaps. Large sizes use numeric glyph coverage for the speed readout. The browser simulator reads the same font plan. The connection header uses an 18 px label with a 10 px state dot. A fresh controller fault or temperature warning takes priority over the connection label and uses the danger colour. Otherwise show the most specific connection/probe/telemetry state; a phone reserving the link is an expected operational state. Ignore expired fault text, and keep an unpaired display labelled Disconnected rather than replacing it with a generic telemetry message. Long header text may wrap within the fixed 48 px row, while the Settings cog keeps its own hit area. Prominent page titles use 24 or 28 px. Metric values reduce to 18 px in compact landscape layouts so large readings retain their units. The speed readout scales with the gauge diameter, with units beneath it. Use ° for angles and °C / °F for temperatures. Speed and temperature units have independent preferences. Header and navigation The top row contains connection state on the left and the settings cog on the right. Vertically centre both controls. Bound long status text to the available width and elide it before it overlaps the cog. The header has no vehicle wordmark or fallback brand heading. Integration identity remains available on the Bike page; its verified capabilities determine the available controls. The bottom navigation contains Ride, Tune and Bike. Use cyan for the active item and a quieter surface for inactive items. Primary-page swipes keep the header and navigation stationary. Cards and controls Use shared Action , IconAction , Metric , SettingsRow and InfoRow components. Prefer consistent padding and spacing over per-page approximations. Button outlines are 2 px, using Theme.control-border . Compact metric cards use 8 px padding; larger cards retain their standard padding. A settings row has a clear label, current value and a chevron icon. Disabled controls use subdued text and borders and reject pointer input. Ride presets, limit steppers and Apply actions are unavailable without a connected bike. Sending changes also requires fresh, stationary telemetry and the normal access checks. Speed gauge Use the gold speed arc over a dark grey track. The stroke scales from a 16 px reference at a 260 px diameter. The speed digits scale to 28% of the diameter and stay centred above the unit label. Portrait layouts use the space recovered from the single-row connection header for a larger gauge, with the battery meter below it. Anchor metric cards above the bottom navigation. Keep status messages in the header; do not add a second status line to the Ride body. Do not solve a clipped power value by removing its unit or shortening it to an ellipsis. Responsive layout and touch The supported rotations are 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°. Portrait and landscape layouts share components and theme tokens. A rotation cancels the current gesture and requires a new contact. Read the panel resolution and safe inset from the hardware profile. Keep header controls, cards, dialogs, keyboard keys and PIN entry inside SafeArea . Rounded panels need additional corner clearance; rectangular panels can use the smaller inset defined by their profile. Check both supported display sizes in both aspect ratios: 410 × 502, 502 × 410, 480 × 800 and 800 × 480. Include long connection text, large power values, independent units, scrolling, PIN entry and keyboard editing. Motion and overlays Primary page transitions use the shared page deck and a 250 ms easing curve. Swipes follow the finger, snap back when cancelled and stop at the first and last page. Vertical scrolling must remain distinct from horizontal navigation. Dialogs and PIN entry block the controls underneath. A sleeping locked screen is black. The wake contact restores the configured brightness and is consumed before another action is accepted. Documentation and web tools The documentation site, simulator shell and firmware installer share the stylesheet, Roboto font, navigation and design tokens in docs/site/ . Their app branding is Wheelie Fun Display . The shared bike artwork in docs/site/bike.svg depicts a 1970s-style minibike. Its orientation flips the bike horizontally, then rotates it 30° clockwise; headers and the favicon use the same oriented geometry. Documentation screenshots use deterministic, connected-looking values generated during the Pages build. They are illustrative previews; the interactive simulator identifies its hardware and services as simulated outside the display canvas. Keep third-party font, icon and artwork notices with the relevant assets. This guide does not change their licenses. "}, {"title": "Third-party notices", "url": "reference/notices/", "description": "Licenses and notices for bundled components and assets.", "text": " Slint runtime/compiler: techfoundrynz/slint mcu-v1.19.5 , commit 8076b03255964faf155c06a38eac6d797d0d2140 . Original notices and license options are preserved in firmware/components/slint_runtime/ . Embedded use requires the applicable GPLv3 or commercial Slint terms; the desktop royalty-free option excludes embedded systems. The original project source is GPL-3.0-only; third-party notices and font licenses remain applicable. VESC wire verification: upstream commands.c , COMM_GET_VALUES_SETUP speed uses a signed integer scaled by 1000. VESC Tool BLE implementation confirms NUS UUIDs. These were consulted as protocol references; their implementation code is not copied. Fonts: Roboto and Sonsie One from google/fonts , under SIL Open Font License 1.1. Font files and respective OFL notices are in ui/fonts/ . The docs/wheelie-*.md files contain WFB protocol and visual design references. Entries marked inferred or unconfirmed require device verification. The 2.06-inch CO5300 commands, direct GPIO reset, 22-pixel column offset and FT3168 wiring were checked against the Waveshare reference . Hardware uses 32 MB flash and 8 MB octal PSRAM. The Slint editor setup script stages the pinned language server and maintains shared editor/task conventions. The fork's LSP supplies language support and the live preview runtime. Firmware control icons are from Lucide , with the ISC/MIT license notices preserved. The website minibike in docs/site/bike.svg is original project artwork under this repository's GPL-3.0-only license. ESP Web Tools 10.4.0 powers the browser firmware installer under Apache-2.0. Its pinned distribution and runtime dependency versions are recorded in docs/flasher/vendor-lock.json . The site hosts the unmodified JavaScript and complete bundled license texts under install/vendor/esp-web-tools/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt . Project source . GUITION ST7701S initialization table: adapted from ultramcu/guition-jc4880p4-bsp , under MIT. Original copyright and license are preserved in firmware/main/boards/ . "}];
